# Unlike colony-firewall-control's scriptlet, this one DOES enable things —
# that is the entire reason the package exists. Turning a firewall on is a
# distribution's decision, so it lives in a distribution package that someone
# installs on purpose, not in the upstream package that anyone might (ADR-0007).

_units='colony-firewalld.service colony-firewall-seed.service colony-firewall-nft.service'

# Everyone who logs in at a seat, so the prompt window can reach the daemon.
#
# The control socket is group-owned by colony-firewall, and without membership
# the UI autostarts and greets the user with "permission denied" — which is what
# happened on the first real install. Doing it here rather than leaving it as a
# command in a message is the difference between a firewall people use and one
# they turn off.
#
# Bounded on purpose: uid >= 1000 and below the nobody range, and only accounts
# with a login shell. It does widen who can change firewall rules on a
# multi-user machine; on the single-seat desktop this distribution is for, the
# alternative is a person who cannot answer their own prompts.
_join_group() {
	local user uid shell
	while IFS=: read -r user _ uid _ _ _ shell; do
		(( uid >= 1000 && uid < 65000 )) || continue
		case $shell in */nologin|*/false|'') continue ;; esac
		usermod -aG colony-firewall "$user" 2>/dev/null || true
	done < /etc/passwd
}

post_install() {
	# preset, not enable: it honours 80-colony-firewall.preset, so an admin who
	# dropped a lower-numbered file in /etc/systemd/system-preset/ to keep the
	# firewall off keeps it off across upgrades. `enable` would override them
	# every time this package was reinstalled.
	# shellcheck disable=SC2086
	systemctl preset $_units 2>/dev/null || true
	_join_group

	cat <<'EOF'

Colony Firewall Control will start at the next boot, in this order:

  colony-firewalld       the daemon
  colony-firewall-seed   the twelve `system` rules — DNS, DHCP, NTP, pacman, ssh
  colony-firewall-nft    the filtering table, last

Each step waits on the one before it, so the fail-closed table can never be
loaded without a daemon behind it or without rules in it.

Anything outside those twelve rules — a browser, a chat client — is held until
you answer a prompt. The tray and the window do that; `cfc prompts` does it from
a terminal.

Inbound filtering stays off. Turning it on can strand a machine you reach over
the network: `systemctl enable --now colony-firewall-nft-inbound`, and read
/usr/share/doc/colony-firewall-control/HARDENING.md first.

To start it now instead of at the next boot:
  systemctl start colony-firewalld colony-firewall-seed colony-firewall-nft

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
	# Deliberately does NOT re-run preset. Someone who disabled the firewall on
	# purpose should not find it back on after an update.
	:
}

pre_remove() {
	# shellcheck disable=SC2086
	systemctl disable --now $_units 2>/dev/null || true
	nft delete table inet colony_firewall 2>/dev/null || true
	cat <<'EOF'

Colony Firewall's units are disabled. colony-firewall-control itself is still
installed; remove it too if that is what you meant.

EOF
}
